            <fr:title text="Geometric theory">Geometric theory</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p><html:strong>Geometric theories</html:strong> may be consicely defined as the elephant theories built up via a sequence of <fr:link href="/pie-limit/" title="PIE Limit" uri="https://coln-project.github.io/pie-limit/" display-uri="pie-limit" type="local">product-inserter-equifier limits</fr:link> starting from the object classifier.</html:p>
            <html:p>We expand this to give more intuition.</html:p>
            <html:p>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T]]></fr:tex> is a theory, a <html:strong>simple functional extension</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T]]></fr:tex> is another theory <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T']]></fr:tex> given as the <html:em>inserter</html:em> of two geometric constructs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F,G \colon  T \to  \mathbb {O}]]></fr:tex>. Intuitively, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T']]></fr:tex> is the theory given by freely adding a new morphism between the “objects” <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T]]></fr:tex> is a theory, a <html:strong>simple equational extension</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T]]></fr:tex> is another theory <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T']]></fr:tex> given as the <html:em>equifier</html:em> of two indexed natural transformations <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha , \beta  \colon  F \Rightarrow  G]]></fr:tex>, where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G]]></fr:tex> are geometric constructs. Intuitively, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T']]></fr:tex> is the theory given by adding a new equality between morphisms of “objects”.</html:p>
            <html:p>A <html:strong>geometric theory</html:strong> is a theory built up by a finite sequence <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T_0,\ldots ,T_n]]></fr:tex> of simple functional extensions and simple equational extensions starting from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T_0 = \mathbb {O}^m]]></fr:tex> for some finite <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[m]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>(See <fr:link href="/johnstone-2002-sketches/" title="Sketches of an elephant: a topos theory compendium" uri="https://coln-project.github.io/johnstone-2002-sketches/" display-uri="johnstone-2002-sketches" type="local">Elephant</fr:link> Definition 4.2.7, note that Johnstone gives a slightly different definition, using <html:em>simple geometric quotients</html:em> in place of simple equational extensions, which use inverters instead of equifiers. The name “simple equational extension” is not known to us to be previously used, but it follows the schema of “simple functional extension.”)</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
